<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction of DDVV-type inequality</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A DDVV-type inequality is an estimate of the form</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum^{m}_{r,s=1}\|[B_{r},B_{s}]\|^{2}\leq c\left(\sum^{m}_{r=1}\|B_{r}\|^{2}\right)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum^{m}_{r,s=1}\|[B_{r},B_{s}]\|^{2}\leq c\left(\sum^{m}_{r=1}\|B_{r}\|^{2}\right)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">considered for certain type of <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrices <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\ldots,B_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\ldots,B_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[A,B]:=AB-BA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[A,B]:=AB-BA</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the commutator, <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|B\|^{2}:=\operatorname{tr}(BB^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|B\|^{2}:=\operatorname{tr}(BB^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the squared Frobenius norm (<math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the conjugate transpose)
and <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is a nonnegative constant.
The DDVV-type inequality originates from the normal scalar curvature conjecture
(which is also called the DDVV conjecture) in submanifold geometry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In 1999, De Smet-Dillen-Verstraelen-Vrancken <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>
proposed the normal scalar curvature conjecture:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(DDVV Conjecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be an immersed submanifold of a real space form with constant sectional curvature <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho+\rho^{\bot}\leq|H|^{2}+\kappa," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho+\rho^{\bot}\leq|H|^{2}+\kappa,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the normalized scalar curvature,
<math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{\bot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{\bot}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the normalized normal scalar curvature
and <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the normalized mean curvature vector field.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Several years later, Dillen-Fastenakels-Veken <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> pointed out that
the geometric inequality (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In Conjecture 1.1 (DDVV Conjecture []). ‣ 1. Introduction of DDVV-type inequality ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) is ture if the algebraic inequality (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction of DDVV-type inequality ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>)
holds for arbitrary <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> real symmetric matrices <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\ldots,B_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\ldots,B_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>
with the universal constant <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1</annotation></semantics></math> (which is called the DDVV inequality).
After some partial results were obtained <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>,
this inequality on real symmetric matrices was finally proved
by Lu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> and Ge-Tang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> independently and differently.
Here, we state their theorem as follows.
Define an action of <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(n,m):=\operatorname{O}(n)\times\operatorname{O}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(n,m):=\operatorname{O}(n)\times\operatorname{O}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>
on a family of matrices <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B_{1},\cdots,B_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B_{1},\cdots,B_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P,R)\cdot(B_{1},\cdots,B_{m}):=(P^{*}B_{1}P,\cdots,P^{*}B_{m}P)\cdot R." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P,R)\cdot(B_{1},\cdots,B_{m}):=(P^{*}B_{1}P,\cdots,P^{*}B_{m}P)\cdot R.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(DDVV inequality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\cdots,B_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\cdots,B_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be arbitrary <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> real symmetric matrices <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n\geq 2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n\geq 2)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</span></p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum^{m}_{r,s=1}\|\left[B_{r},B_{s}\right]\|^{2}\leq\left(\sum^{m}_{r=1}\|B_{r}\|^{2}\right)^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum^{m}_{r,s=1}\|\left[B_{r},B_{s}\right]\|^{2}\leq\left(\sum^{m}_{r=1}\|B_{r}\|^{2}\right)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The equality holds if and only if there exists a <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P,R)\in K(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P,R)\in K(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P,R)\cdot(B_{1},\cdots,B_{m})=(\diag(H_{1},0),\diag(H_{2},0),0,\cdots,0)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P,R)\cdot(B_{1},\cdots,B_{m})=(\diag(H_{1},0),\diag(H_{2},0),0,\cdots,0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where for some <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}:=\begin{pmatrix}\lambda&amp;0\\
0&amp;-\lambda\\
\end{pmatrix},\ H_{2}:=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;\lambda\\
\lambda&amp;0\\
\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>λ</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi>λ</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>λ</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}:=\begin{pmatrix}\lambda&amp;0\\
0&amp;-\lambda\\
\end{pmatrix},\ H_{2}:=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;\lambda\\
\lambda&amp;0\\
\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A natural question is to determine the optimal universal constant <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction of DDVV-type inequality ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>)
when the matrices <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\ldots,B_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\ldots,B_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are in other regarded classes.
Using the same approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>,
Ge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> proved the DDVV-type inequality for real skew-symmetric matrices,
and applied it to Yang-Mills fields in Riemannian submersion geometry.
Subsequently, Ge-Xu-You-Zhou <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> extended the DDVV-type inequalities from real symmetric and skew-symmetric matrices to Hermitian and skew-Hermitian matrices.
Further, by decomposing complex matrices into Hermitian matrices and skew-Hermitian matrices,
Ge-Li-Zhou <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> generalized the DDVV-type inequalities for complex (skew-)symmetric matrices
and general complex (real) matrices.
According to the same philosophy,
Ge-Li-Zhou <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> also obtained the DDVV-type inequality for general quaternionic matrices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In 2005, Böttcher-Wenzel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> raised the so-called BW conjecture:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(BW conjecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmthm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y</annotation></semantics></math> be arbitrary <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> real matrices, then</span></p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\left[X,Y\right]\|^{2}\leq 2\|X\|^{2}\|Y\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\left[X,Y\right]\|^{2}\leq 2\|X\|^{2}\|Y\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\|X\|^{2}\|Y\|^{2}\leq(\|X\|^{2}+\|Y\|^{2})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\|X\|^{2}\|Y\|^{2}\leq(\|X\|^{2}+\|Y\|^{2})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the above BW inequality <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(</annotation></semantics></math><a href="#S1.E4" title="In Conjecture 1.3 (BW conjecture []). ‣ 1. Introduction of DDVV-type inequality ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a><math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext=")" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">)</annotation></semantics></math> implies the DDVV inequality for the case <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Böttcher-Wenzel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and Làszlò <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> first proved
the special case of <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
And then the complete proofs were given by Böttcher-Wenzel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, Vong-Jin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite>, Audenaert <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and Lu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> in various ways.
Böttcher-Wenzel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> also extended the BW inequality from real matrices to complex matrices,
and Cheng-Vong-Wenzel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> obtained the characterization of the equality.
By introducing the Kronecker product, Ge-Li-Lu-Zhou <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> obtained new proofs
of the complex BW inequality and its equality condition.
For the convenience of readers, we restate the BW inequality and the equality condition in the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(BW inequality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmthm4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y</annotation></semantics></math> be arbitrary real (or complex) matrices, then</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\left[X,Y\right]\|^{2}\leq 2\|X\|^{2}\|Y\|^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\left[X,Y\right]\|^{2}\leq 2\|X\|^{2}\|Y\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the equality holds if and only if there exists a unitary matrix <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=U(X_{0}\oplus O)U^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=U(X_{0}\oplus O)U^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=U(Y_{0}\oplus O)U^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=U(Y_{0}\oplus O)U^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
with a maximal pair <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},Y_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},Y_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> matrices. Here, <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the zero matrix in corresponding order, two matrices <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y\in\mathbb{C}^{2\times 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y\in\mathbb{C}^{2\times 2}</annotation></semantics></math> form a maximal pair if and only if <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{tr}X=\operatorname{tr}Y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{tr}X=\operatorname{tr}Y=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\langle X,Y\right\rangle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\langle X,Y\right\rangle=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Also, analogues with, e.g., Schatten norms
were investigated (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>, <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>, etc).
In addition,
Ge-Li-Zhou <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> generalized the BW inequality from complex matrices to quaternionic matrices.
Some other generalizations of the BW-type inequalities were also obtained by
Fong-Cheng-Lok<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, Wenzel<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite>, Wenzel-Audenaert <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>,
Cheng-Fong-Lei <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, Cheng-Liang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, Cheng-Akintoye-Jiao <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, Làszlò<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> and Chruściński-Kimura-Ohno-Singal<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, etc. For more details, we recommend <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> for a comprehensive overview on the developments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize the optimal constant <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> for DDVV-type inequalities and BW-type inequalities mentioned above in the following Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1. Introduction of DDVV-type inequality ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Table <a href="#S1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 1. Introduction of DDVV-type inequality ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
