<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>The origin and applications in geometry</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>DDVV inequality in submanifold geometry</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an immersed submanifold of a real space form <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{n+m}(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{n+m}(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>
with constant sectional curvature <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be the Riemannian curvature tensor of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, let
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bot}</annotation></semantics></math> be the curvature tensor of the normal connection,
and let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{II}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>II</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{II}</annotation></semantics></math> be the second fundamental form.
For an arbitrary point <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in M</annotation></semantics></math>,
let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{1},\cdots,e_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{1},\cdots,e_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\xi_{1},\cdots,\xi_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\xi_{1},\cdots,\xi_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be orthonormal bases of
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}^{\bot}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}^{\bot}M</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
Then the normalized scalar curvature is</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{2}{n(n-1)}\sum_{1=i&lt;j}^{n}\langle R(e_{i},e_{j})e_{j},e_{i}\rangle," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{2}{n(n-1)}\sum_{1=i&lt;j}^{n}\langle R(e_{i},e_{j})e_{j},e_{i}\rangle,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">the normalized normal scalar curvature is</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{\bot}=\frac{2}{n(n-1)}\Bigg(\sum_{1=i&lt;j}^{n}\sum_{1=r&lt;s}^{m}\langle R^{\bot}(e_{i},e_{j})\xi_{r},\xi_{s}\rangle^{2}\Bigg)^{\frac{1}{2}}=\frac{2}{n(n-1)}|R^{\bot}|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{\bot}=\frac{2}{n(n-1)}\Bigg(\sum_{1=i&lt;j}^{n}\sum_{1=r&lt;s}^{m}\langle R^{\bot}(e_{i},e_{j})\xi_{r},\xi_{s}\rangle^{2}\Bigg)^{\frac{1}{2}}=\frac{2}{n(n-1)}|R^{\bot}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and the normalized mean curvature vector field is
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\operatorname{II}(e_{i},e_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>II</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\operatorname{II}(e_{i},e_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq r\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq r\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>,
let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be the matrix correspond to the shape operator in direction <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>
with respect to the basis <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{1},\cdots,e_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{1},\cdots,e_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{r}:=A_{r}-\langle H,\xi_{r}\rangle I_{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{r}:=A_{r}-\langle H,\xi_{r}\rangle I_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">On the one hand, by the Gauss equation, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-n(n-1)(\rho-\kappa)=|\operatorname{II}|^{2}-n^{2}|H|^{2}=\sum^{m}_{r=1}\|B_{r}\|^{2}-n(n-1)|H|^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>II</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-n(n-1)(\rho-\kappa)=|\operatorname{II}|^{2}-n^{2}|H|^{2}=\sum^{m}_{r=1}\|B_{r}\|^{2}-n(n-1)|H|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">and thus</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|H|^{2}-\rho+\kappa=\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\sum^{m}_{r=1}\|B_{r}\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|H|^{2}-\rho+\kappa=\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\sum^{m}_{r=1}\|B_{r}\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.6" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, by the Ricci equation, we have</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{\bot}=\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\Bigg(\sum^{m}_{r,s=1}\|[A_{r},A_{s}]\|^{2}\Bigg)^{\frac{1}{2}}=\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\Bigg(\sum^{m}_{r,s=1}\|[B_{r},B_{s}]\|^{2}\Bigg)^{\frac{1}{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{\bot}=\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\Bigg(\sum^{m}_{r,s=1}\|[A_{r},A_{s}]\|^{2}\Bigg)^{\frac{1}{2}}=\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\Bigg(\sum^{m}_{r,s=1}\|[B_{r},B_{s}]\|^{2}\Bigg)^{\frac{1}{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.7" class="ltx_p">It follows from (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1. DDVV inequality in submanifold geometry ‣ 2. The origin and applications in geometry ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1. DDVV inequality in submanifold geometry ‣ 2. The origin and applications in geometry ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>) that
the geometric DDVV inequality (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In Conjecture 1.1 (DDVV Conjecture []). ‣ 1. Introduction of DDVV-type inequality ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) can be derived from
the algebraic DDVV inequality (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In Theorem 1.2 (DDVV inequality []). ‣ 1. Introduction of DDVV-type inequality ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>).
Therefore, Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2 (DDVV inequality []). ‣ 1. Introduction of DDVV-type inequality ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> implies the following normal scalar curvature inequality:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Ge-Tang<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, Lu<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be an immersed submanifold of a real space form <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{n+m}(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{n+m}(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho+\rho^{\bot}\leq|H|^{2}+\kappa." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho+\rho^{\bot}\leq|H|^{2}+\kappa.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The equality holds at some point <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in M</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there exist an orthonormal basis <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{1},\cdots,e_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{1},\cdots,e_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}M</annotation></semantics></math> and an orthonormal basis <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\xi_{1},\cdots,\xi_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\xi_{1},\cdots,\xi_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}^{\bot}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}^{\bot}M</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}=\lambda_{1}I_{n}+\mu\diag(H_{1},0),\ A_{2}=\lambda_{2}I_{n}+\mu\diag(H_{2},0),\ A_{3}=\lambda_{3}I_{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}=\lambda_{1}I_{n}+\mu\diag(H_{1},0),\ A_{2}=\lambda_{2}I_{n}+\mu\diag(H_{2},0),\ A_{3}=\lambda_{3}I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\xi_{r}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\xi_{r}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;3</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu,\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\lambda_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu,\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\lambda_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are real numbers.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are many generalized normal scalar curvature inequalities under different geometric assumptions
such as special submanifolds in statistical manifolds, complex space forms or Sasakian space forms
(cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>, <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The DDVV inequality has many other important applications in submanifold geometry.
For example,
it was used in Gu-Xu’s work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> on Yau rigidity theorem for minimal submanifolds in spheres.
The case of two symmetric matrices is a core technology in the proof of
the following integral inequality by Chern-do Carmo-Kobayashi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.
To simplify notations, we denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> the squared length of the second fundamental form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Chern-do Carmo-Kobayashi<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, Simons<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed, minimal, immersed submanifold of a real space form <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{n+m}(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{n+m}(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{M}\left[\left(2-\frac{1}{m}\right)S-n\kappa\right]S\ dV_{M}\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{M}\left[\left(2-\frac{1}{m}\right)S-n\kappa\right]S\ dV_{M}\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The above integral inequality started a series of explorations on the gap phenomena and pinching results
for the second fundamental form (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>, <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>).
Finally, we have the following pinching theorem:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Chern-do Carmo-Kobayashi<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, Lawson<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a minimal hypersurface in the unit sphere <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}^{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}^{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq S\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq S\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is either totally geodesic or is one of the Clifford tori</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{k,n-k}=\mathbf{S}^{k}\left(\sqrt{\frac{k}{n}}\,\right)\times\mathbf{S}^{n-k}\left(\sqrt{\frac{n-k}{n}}\,\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{k,n-k}=\mathbf{S}^{k}\left(\sqrt{\frac{k}{n}}\,\right)\times\mathbf{S}^{n-k}\left(\sqrt{\frac{n-k}{n}}\,\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Chen-Xu<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, Li-Li<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed, minimal submanifold in the unit sphere <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}^{n+m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}^{n+m}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq S\leq\frac{2}{3}n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq S\leq\frac{2}{3}n</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is either totally geodesic or is a Veronese surface in <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}^{2+m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}^{2+m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, Lu considered
the fundamental matrix on <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> which is an <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix-valued function defined as <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(a_{rs})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(a_{rs})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{rs}=\langle A_{r},A_{s}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{rs}=\langle A_{r},A_{s}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{m}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{m}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> be the eigenvalues of the fundamental matrix.
Note that <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the trace of the fundamental matrix, i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\lambda_{1}+\cdots+\lambda_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\lambda_{1}+\cdots+\lambda_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}:=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}:=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following result with pinching quantity <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S+\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S+\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> was proved
by using a generalized DDVV inequality (see Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm11" title="Theorem 3.11 (Lu inequality []). ‣ 3.4. Lu inequality ‣ 3. New progress on DDVV-type inequalities ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lu<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed minimal submanifold in the unit sphere <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}^{n+m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}^{n+m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq S+\lambda_{2}\leq n," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq S+\lambda_{2}\leq n,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is totally geodesic, or is one of the Clifford tori <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{k,n-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{k,n-k}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1\leq k&lt;n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1\leq k&lt;n)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}^{n+m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}^{n+m}</annotation></semantics></math>,
or is a Veronese surface in <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}^{2+m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}^{2+m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}\leq\frac{1}{2}S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}\leq\frac{1}{2}S</annotation></semantics></math>, the above Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Lu[]). ‣ 2.1. DDVV inequality in submanifold geometry ‣ 2. The origin and applications in geometry ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> extended the former rigidity results
(Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 2.3 (Chern-do Carmo-Kobayashi[], Lawson[]). ‣ 2.1. DDVV inequality in submanifold geometry ‣ 2. The origin and applications in geometry ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 2.4 (Chen-Xu[], Li-Li[]). ‣ 2.1. DDVV inequality in submanifold geometry ‣ 2. The origin and applications in geometry ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>).
Furthermore, Leng-Xu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> generalized Lu’s rigidity theorem to submanifolds with parallel mean curvature.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Simons-type integral inequality in Riemannian submersion geometry</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In some sense, Riemannian submersions can be seen as the “dual” of isometric immersions,
while real skew-symmetric matrices can also be seen as a kind of “dual” of real symmetric matrices.
Fortunately, analogous to the case of real symmetric matrices,
the DDVV-type inequality for real skew-symmetric matrices can also deduce
a Simons-type integral inequality for Riemannian submersions.
In fact, Ge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> applied it to give a Simons-type inequality for (generalized) Yang-Mills fields in Riemannian submersions geometry (dual to Simons inequality for minimal submanifolds of spheres in submanifold geometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>). The dual phenomenon between Yang-Mills fields and minimal submanifolds was initially investigated by Tian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:M^{n+m}\rightarrow B^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:M^{n+m}\rightarrow B^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Riemannian submersion,
and let <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be the Levi-Civita connection on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the O’Neill integrability tensor <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{X}Y:=\mathscr{H}D_{\mathscr{H}X}\mathscr{V}Y+\mathscr{V}D_{\mathscr{H}X}\mathscr{H}Y," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{X}Y:=\mathscr{H}D_{\mathscr{H}X}\mathscr{V}Y+\mathscr{V}D_{\mathscr{H}X}\mathscr{H}Y,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{V}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the projections from the tangent bundle <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TM</annotation></semantics></math> to the horizontal and vertical distribution, respectively. <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is essentially the curvature when the Riemannian submersion is a Euclidean vector bundle projection, and thus a similar equation <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{D}A=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{D}A=0</annotation></semantics></math> about <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is used to define a Yang-Mills horizontal distribution as Yang-Mills connections on Euclidean vector bundles.
Since the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-tensor field <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is alternating on the horizontal distribution, locally it can be represented by <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> skew-symmetric matrices of order <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
We first state the DDVV-type inequality for real skew-symmetric matrices:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Ge<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\cdots,B_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\cdots,B_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> real skew-symmetric matrices.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

