<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some unit column vector <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathbb{H}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathbb{H}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1},q_{2},q_{3}\in\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1},q_{2},q_{3}\in\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math> are orthogonal imaginary quaternions with the same norm.
Also note that one can try to tackle the transition from the real to complex matrices in a similar, natural way. However, the doubled constant turns out not to be sharp in this case.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
