<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>DDVV-type inequality for Clifford system and Clifford algebra</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by the relation between DDVV-type inequalities and Erdős-Mordell inequality (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, etc) discovered by Z. Lu,
Ge-Li-Zhou <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> established the DDVV-type inequalities for matrices in the subspaces spanned by a Clifford system or a Clifford algebra.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate the number <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> more explicitly, we briefly introduce the representation theory of Clifford algebra (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>). A Clifford system on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2l}</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented by real symmetric orthogonal matrices <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0},\cdots,P_{m}\in O(2l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0},\cdots,P_{m}\in O(2l)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}P_{j}+P_{j}P_{i}=2\delta_{ij}I_{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}P_{j}+P_{j}P_{i}=2\delta_{ij}I_{2l}</annotation></semantics></math>; a Clifford algebra on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented by real skew-symmetric orthogonal matrices <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1},\cdots,E_{m-1}\in O(l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1},\cdots,E_{m-1}\in O(l)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}E_{j}+E_{j}E_{i}=-2\delta_{ij}I_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}E_{j}+E_{j}E_{i}=-2\delta_{ij}I_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>; they are one-to-one correspondent by setting</p>
<table id="S3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{0}=\begin{pmatrix}I_{l}&amp;0\\
0&amp;-I_{l}\end{pmatrix},\quad P_{1}=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;I_{l}\\
I_{l}&amp;0\end{pmatrix},\quad P_{\alpha+1}=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;E_{\alpha}\\
-E_{\alpha}&amp;0\end{pmatrix},\quad\alpha=1,\cdots,m-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}=\begin{pmatrix}I_{l}&amp;0\\
0&amp;-I_{l}\end{pmatrix},\quad P_{1}=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;I_{l}\\
I_{l}&amp;0\end{pmatrix},\quad P_{\alpha+1}=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;E_{\alpha}\\
-E_{\alpha}&amp;0\end{pmatrix},\quad\alpha=1,\cdots,m-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">A Clifford system <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{0},\cdots,P_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{0},\cdots,P_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2l}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. Clifford algebra <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E_{1},\cdots,E_{m-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E_{1},\cdots,E_{m-1})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math>) can be decomposed into a direct sum of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> irreducible Clifford systems (resp. Clifford algebras) on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2\delta(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2\delta(m)}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{\delta(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{\delta(m)}</annotation></semantics></math>) with <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=k\delta(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=k\delta(m)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,m\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,m\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the irreducible dimension <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(m)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(m+8)=16\delta(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(m+8)=16\delta(m)</annotation></semantics></math> and can be listed in the following Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.3. DDVV-type inequality for Clifford system and Clifford algebra ‣ 3. New progress on DDVV-type inequalities ‣ A survey on the DDVV-type inequalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
